.TH OPEN 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
open \- open or create a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fcntl.h>
.PP
.BI "int open(const char* " path ", int " flags ", mode_t " mode ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR open ()
opens the NUL\-terminated path and returns a file descriptor.
All three arguments are required.
.I flags
contains one access mode,
.BR O_RDONLY ,
.B O_WRONLY
or
.BR O_RDWR ,
optionally combined using bitwise OR with
.BR O_CREAT ,
.BR O_EXCL ,
.BR O_TRUNC ,
or
.BR O_APPEND .
Their meanings are the Linux
.BR openat (2)
flag meanings.
.PP
When a file is created,
.I mode
specifies the numeric permission bits, modified by the process umask.
For example, 420 specifies permissions equivalent to Unix 0644.
Pass 0 when creation permissions are not needed.
Existing files are not changed by the mode argument.
.PP
The header also declares
.BR STDIN_FILENO ,
.BR STDOUT_FILENO ,
and
.BR STDERR_FILENO
for descriptors 0, 1, and 2.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The new descriptor is returned on success.
On failure, \-1 is returned and
.B errno
is set to the positive Linux error number.
Successful calls leave
.B errno
unchanged.
.SH ERRORS
Include
.B <errno.h>
for error constants and the writable
.BR errno .
Possible errors include
.BR EACCES ,
.BR EEXIST ,
.BR EINVAL ,
.BR EISDIR ,
and
.BR ENOENT .
Other Linux
.BR openat (2)
errors are possible.
.SH NOTES
This is a Crunch library function, not a variadic C prototype.
Its implementation is linked from
.BR libcrunch.a ;
.B errno
is process\-wide, not thread\-local.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR close (3crunch),
.BR read (3crunch),
.BR write (3crunch),
.BR openat (2)
